Chloropomp erupted in the early 2000s between the crumbling Victorian hothouses of Glasgow and the botanical field stations along Brazil’s Rio Negro. A cadre of rogue ethnobotanists and disgraced conservatory violinists began wiring silver-chloride microelectrodes directly into the vascular tissue of giant monsteras and strangler figs, patching plant-action potentials directly into patched Access Virus synthesizers and drum racks. The subculture—calling themselves "Vessel Dandies"—parades in damp velvet tailcoats draped with living moss, 20-hole steel-toe combat boots caked in red clay, and theatrical white face powder stained with crushed chlorophyll eye paint. Performance rituals resemble secular, sweaty hothouse séances: audiences bring greenhouse cuttings to wire into the master modular rig, dancing until bodily condensation drips from ceiling glass to complete the circuit.

